course: dictionary
lesson: 3993
title: Galvanise: shock into action, or coat with zinc
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. Galvanise is a good example. Read the words around it to find out which meaning the writer wants. «The news of the flood galvanised the whole town into action.» Here people were shocked and excited, so they all started to work quickly together.
explain: Galvanise can also be a science word for coating iron or steel with zinc so it does not rust. «Paint stops rust until it is scratched; galvanised steel has a zinc coat that protects the iron even at a scratch.» The clues are steel, zinc and rust, so this meaning fits.
explain: Check by swapping your meaning into the sentence. If galvanised means shocked into action, does «The news of the flood shocked the whole town into action» still make sense? Yes, so you have found the right meaning. Use the clues to choose, not the first meaning you remember.
fact: Galvanise has two common meanings: to shock or excite people into acting, and to coat iron or steel with zinc.
fact: «Galvanised» steel is coated with zinc, so it does not rust easily.
fact: The zinc coat still protects the iron even when the surface is scratched.
fact: Galvanise into action means to make people start doing something quickly.
fact: Long ago, galvanise could also mean to give something an electric shock, from the scientist Luigi Galvani.
exercise: «The news of the flood galvanised the whole town into action.» What does galvanised mean here? | Made to sleep for a long time | Lost in a fog | *Shocked and excited into acting quickly | Painted bright red
exercise: «The bucket was galvanised, so it did not rust.» Why did the bucket not rust? | It was left out in the rain on purpose | It was painted with a thick coat of blue paint | *It was coated with zinc to protect the iron | It was made of wood
exercise: Find the word that means to coat iron or steel with zinc so it does not rust. | *galvanise | bucket | fence | rust
test: Mum says the sudden fire alarm galvanised everyone out of the building. What does galvanised mean? | Made very wet | Fed up and bored | *Shocked into moving quickly | Covered in paint
test: Why is galvanised steel used for fences? | *Its zinc coat stops rust, even when it is scratched | It is soft and bendy | It glows in the dark | It is warm to touch
exam: In an old science book, galvanise is used to mean something different: what did it once mean? | To count it carefully | To paint it blue | *To give something an electric shock | To make it warm
